<h2>The principle: means include capacity and conduct</h2>
<p>Section 144 BNSS (the successor to Section 125 CrPC), Section 24 HMA and Section 20 of the DV Act all speak of the respondent\'s means. Means has never been read as the number on Form 16. It includes earning capacity, assets, and the inference that a person who spends at a certain level earns at least at that level. The burden-shifting is practical: once the claimant shows a lifestyle inconsistent with the declared income, the respondent must explain the gap — gifts, loans, family support — with evidence, not assertion.</p>

<h2>What to collect, head by head</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Housing and vehicles</strong> — rent agreements or ownership records of the residence, society maintenance charges, vehicle registrations (obtainable from transport authorities), insurance premiums, EMI trails.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Education and staff</strong> — children\'s school fee receipts, tuition and activity invoices, salaries of domestic staff and drivers; these are recurring, documented and hard to disown.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Travel</strong> — passport stamps, boarding passes, visa records, hotel bookings, and travel photographs; international travel is among the strongest lifestyle indicators.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Cards and wallets</strong> — credit card statements and UPI histories summoned through court show actual monthly burn better than any affidavit.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Business signals</strong> — GST filings, business premises rent, staff strength, trade association memberships, and the respondent\'s own marketing material boasting of turnover.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Digital footprint</strong> — public social media content, duly preserved and certified as electronic evidence, contradicting claimed hardship.</div>
</div>

<h2>Using the material in court</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Plead the lifestyle</strong> — the petition and the claimant\'s own Rajnesh affidavit should narrate the standard of living during cohabitation with particulars, not adjectives.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Force disclosure</strong> — seek directions for bank statements, ITRs, GST returns and card statements over a meaningful period, and summon third-party records where suppression is likely.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Confront the affidavit</strong> — map each documented expense against the respondent\'s declared income; the unexplained residue is the case.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Ask for notional income</strong> — invite the court to attribute income consistent with the proven spending, and to draw an adverse inference from any refusal to disclose.</div>
</div>

<h2>Defending against lifestyle claims</h2>
<p>Respondents with genuine reverses are not defenceless. Document the downturn — business closure papers, loan recalls, medical events — and explain visible spending with its true source: an employer-provided car, a sibling\'s sponsorship of a family event, accumulated savings now depleted. Courts distinguish between a lifestyle funded by current income and the afterglow of better years; the distinction must, however, be proved. Vague pleas of unemployment from an able-bodied professional attract notional income on earning capacity, lifestyle evidence or none.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Maintenance adjudication is ultimately an exercise in reconstructing financial truth from fragments. The side that arrives with a documented, itemised picture — of what the family spent, on what, and from where — almost always frames the court\'s view of what the other side can pay. Lifestyle evidence is not a substitute for the statutory affidavit; it is the audit that tests it.</p>
</div>
